1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Farrukhan_the_Great '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Farrukhan the Great''' (in Persian: فرخان بزرگ, Farrukhan-e Bozorg; 712 - 728) was the independent ruler (ispahbadh) of Tabaristan in the early 8th century, until his death in 728. He is the first actually attested (through his
2 CONC  coinage) member of the Dabuyid dynasty, which is traditionally held to have ruled Tabaristan since the time of the Muslim conquest of Persia. He maintained his independence against the attacks of the Umayyad Caliphate, and was su
2 CONC cceeded by his eldest son, Dadhburzmihr.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Family
2 CONT * 2 Contributions
2 CONT * 3 References
2 CONT * 4 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT =Family=
2 CONT
2 CONT Farrukhan had 3 sons, who were named Saruyih, Farrukhan the Little and Dadhburzmihr.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contributions=
2 CONT
2 CONT During his early rule, he reconstructed the city of Zadracarta and made it his capital, and because his son's name was "Saruyih", he called it by this name, which later became known as Sari.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Pourshariati (2008), pp. 471
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Madelung, W. (1975). "The Minor Dynasties of Northern Iran". In Frye, R.N. The Cambridge History of Iran, Volume 4: From the Arab Invasion to the Saljuqs. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press. pp. 198–249. ISBN 978-0-521-20093-
2 CONC 6.
2 CONT * Madelung, Wilferd (1993). "DABUYIDS". In Yarshater, Ehsan. Encyclopaedia Iranica, Vol. VI, Fasc. 5. London et al.: Routledge & Kegan Paul. pp. 541–544. ISBN 1-56859-007-5.
2 CONT * Pourshariati, Parvaneh (2008). Decline and Fall of the Sasanian Empire: The Sasanian-Parthian Confederacy and the Arab Conquest of Iran. London and New York: I.B. Tauris. ISBN 978-1-84511-645-3.
