2 NICK the Deaf
1 NAME Farrukhan the Little
1 SEX M
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Farrukhan_the_Little '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Farrukhan the Little''' (Persian: Farrukhan-e Kuchak), also surnamed '''the Deaf''' (Korbali),[1] was a member of the Dabuyid dynasty, which ruled Tabaristan as independent monarchs in the century after the Muslim conquest of P
2 CONC ersia. The brother of the ispahbadh Dadhburzmihr, Farrukhan governed Tabaristan between 740/41 and 747/48 as regent for his underage nephew, Khurshid.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT Farrukhan was the younger son of Farrukhan the Great (died ca. 728), the first ruler (ispahbadh) of the Dabuyid dynasty from whose reign coins are known.[2][1][3] According to the traditional account, the Dabuyids had established 
2 CONC themselves as the quasi-independent rulers of Tabaristan in the 640s, during the tumults of the Muslim conquest of Persia and the collapse of the Sassanid Empire. They owed only the payment tribute and nominal vassalage to the Ara
2 CONC b Caliphate, and managed, despite repeated Muslim attempts at invasion, to maintain their autonomy by exploiting the inaccessible terrain of their country.[1][4] A more recent interpretation of the sources by P. Pourshariati, howe
2 CONC ver, supports that Farrukhan the Great was the one who actually established the family's rule over Tabaristan, sometime in the 670s.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Farrukhan the Great was succeeded by his eldest son, Dadhburzmihr, who died in 740/41. He was succeeded by his son, Khurshid, but he was only six years old. Shortly before his death, Dadhburzmihr appointed Farrukhan the Little as 
2 CONC regent until Khurshid came of age. Farrukhan effectively ruled Tabaristan for the next eight years, but this is not reflected in the coinage of the period, with coins struck solely in Khurshid's name from 741 on.[1][2] Farrukhan's
2 CONC  regency coincides with recorded attempts by the Dabuyids to strengthen their position vis-á-vis the Umayyad Caliphate; they used the turmoil of the Third Islamic Civil War to rebel against Caliph Marwan II (r. 744–750), and even 
2 CONC sent an embassy to the Tang court in 746, which recognized him ("king Hu-lu-ban") as a vassal prince.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT When Khurshid came of age, Farrukhan prepared to hand over power, but his own sons disagreed and tried to usurp the throne. Their plot was allegedly betrayed to Khurshid by a slave girl, Varmja Haraviya. With the help of the sons 
2 CONC of another cousin, Jushnas, Khurshid managed to defeat and imprison Farrukhan's sons. He later took Varmja Haraviya as his wife, while the sons of Jushnas were given high positions in the state.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Madelung (1993), pp. 541–544
2 CONT # Rekaya (1986), pp. 68–70
2 CONT # Pourshariati (2008), pp. 312–313
2 CONT # Madelung (1975), pp. 198–199
2 CONT # Pourshariati (2008), pp. 308–313
2 CONT # Pourshariati (2008), pp. 313–314
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Madelung, W. (1975). "The Minor Dynasties of Northern Iran". In Frye, R.N. The Cambridge History of Iran, Volume 4: From the Arab Invasion to the Saljuqs. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press. pp. 198–249. ISBN 978-0-521-20093-
2 CONC 6.
2 CONT * Madelung, Wilferd (1993). "DABUYIDS". In Yarshater, Ehsan. Encyclopaedia Iranica, Vol. VI, Fasc. 5. London et al.: Routledge & Kegan Paul. pp. 541–544. ISBN 1-56859-007-5.
2 CONT * Pourshariati, Parvaneh (2008). Decline and Fall of the Sasanian Empire: The Sasanian-Parthian Confederacy and the Arab Conquest of Iran. London and New York: I.B. Tauris. ISBN 978-1-84511-645-3.
2 CONT * Rekaya, M. (1986). "Khurshīd". The Encyclopedia of Islam, New Edition, Volume V: Khe–Mahi. Leiden and New York: BRILL. pp. 68–70. ISBN 90-04-07819-3.
