1 NOTE {geni:about_me} from Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Suleiman bin Qutalmish''' (Old Anatolian Turkish: سُلَیمان بن قُتَلمِش, Persian: سلیمان بن قتلمش‎) founded an independent Seljuq Turkish state in Anatolia and ruled as Seljuq Sultan of Rûm from 1077 until his death in 1086.
2 CONT
2 CONT Suleyman was the son of Qutalmish, who had struggled unsuccessfully against his cousin Alp Arslan for the throne of Great Seljuq Empire. When Kutalmish died in 1064, Suleyman fled with his three brothers into the Taurus Mountains 
2 CONC and there sought refuge with Turkmen tribes living beyond the borders of the empire. Alp Arslan responded by launching a series of punitive expeditions against them. Of the four brothers, Suleyman alone survived the raids and was 
2 CONC able to consolidate his leadership of the Turkmen.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1078, the Byzantine emperor Michael VII sought the help of Suleyman against Nicephorus Botaneiates, the commander of the Anatolic Theme, who had challenged the emperor for the throne. Suleyman intercepted Botaneiates' small for
2 CONC ce between Cotyaeum and Nicaea, whereupon the usurper persuaded Suleyman to join his rebellion by offering him incentives superior to those of the emperor.[2] Nicephorus' bid for power was successful, and in return for their suppo
2 CONC rt Suleyman's Turkmen were allowed to settle on the Asiatic side of the Bosphorus, near Constantinople itself. Two years later, Suleyman lent his support to another pretender, Nicephorus Melissenus.[3] It was the latter Nicephorus
2 CONC  who opened the gates of Nicaea to the Turkmen, allowing Suleyman to establish a permanent base.[4] All Bithynia was soon under Suleyman's control, a circumstance which allowed him to restrict communication between Constantinople 
2 CONC and the former Byzantine subjects in Anatolia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1084, Suleiman left Nicaea, leaving his kinsman Abu'l Qasim in charge.
2 CONT
2 CONT Suleiman expanded his realm, in 1085 he captured Antioch and proceeded to massacre its inhabitants. Moreover, the treasures of the church of St. Cassianus were stolen and the church was converted into a mosque.[5] He was killed ne
2 CONC ar Antioch in 1086 by Tutush I, the Seljuq ruler of Syria. Suleyman's son, Kilij Arslan I, was captured, and Malik Shah transferred him to Isfahan as a hostage. It is uncertain whether Tutush killed Suleiman out of loyalty to Mali
2 CONC k-Shah I or simply for personal gain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the death of Malik-Shah I, Kilij Arslan I re-established the Sultanate of Rûm.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Claude Cahen, Pre-Ottoman Turkey: a general survey of the material and spiritual culture and history c. 1071-1330, trans. J. Jones-Williams (New York: Taplinger, 1968), pp. 73-4.
2 CONT # Speros Vryonis, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor and the Process of Islamization from the Eleventh through the Fifteenth Century (University of California Press, 1971), pp. 112-3.
2 CONT # George Ostrogorsky, History of the Byzantine State, trans. Joan Hussey (Rutgers University Press, 1969), pp. 348-9.
2 CONT # Cahen, p. 75
2 CONT # Speros Vryonis, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor and the Process of Islamization from the Eleventh through the Fifteenth Century (University of California Press, 1971), p. 159
