1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * She is mentioned in the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sam_ibn_Wardanruz '''Wikipedia page'''] of her husband, Sam ibn Wardanruz
2 CONT
2 CONT Sam was appointed as atabeg by Ahmad Sanjar in 1141, where he married one of the daughters of the Kakuyid Garshasp II. He is described as a weak ruler, and was in 1188 replaced by his more capable brother Langar ibn Wardanruz. Sam
2 CONC  later died in 1193.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * from [https://books.google.com/books?id=lQjkJOUcVIwC&pg=PA35&lpg=PA35&dq=Zubeida+Khatun,+wife+of+Malik-Shah&source=bl&ots=5h6pZ2rimG&sig=reGhqSBaBsqKwuYCVdSrdzoi_6I&hl=en&sa=X&ei=vWwyVdW8EMnksAT_mIDwBQ&ved=0CDEQ6AEwAw#v=onepage&
2 CONC q=Aka&f=false '''Continuity and Change in Medieval Persia'''] by Ann K.S. Lambton:
2 CONT
2 CONT There were other marriages between the Saljuqs and the Kakuyids of Yazd. Sitara, the sister of Mohammad b. Malik Shah and Sanjar, was married to Ala al-Daula Garchasp b. Ali b. Faramarz. Garchasp, the son of Arslan Khatun and Ala 
2 CONC al-Daula Ali, was with Sanjar at the battle of Qatvan and was killed in the engagement. '''His two daughters*''' were sent back to Yazd with Rukn al-Din Sam as their atabeg. Garchasp apparently had another daughter Ata Khatun, who
2 CONC  was married to Mahmud b. Muhammad.
2 CONT
2 CONT  *emphasis not present in original text
