2 CONT
2 CONT St Damiane (Demetrius) the King and Hymnographer Orthodox synaxarion
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Demetrius I (დემეტრე I) (c. 1093 – 1156), from the Bagrationi dynasty, was King of Georgia from 1125 to 1156. He is also known as a poet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Demetrius was the eldest son of King David the Builder by his first wife Rusudan. As a commander, he took part in his father’s battles, particularly at Didgori (1121) and Shirvan (1123).
2 CONT
2 CONT Demetrius succeeded on his father’s death on January 24, 1125. With his ascent to the throne, the Seljuk Turks attacked the Georgian-held city of Ani, Armenia. Demetrius I had to compromise and ceded the city to a Seljuk ruler und
2 CONC er terms of vassalage.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1139, he raided the earthquake-ridden city of Ganja in Arran (the present day Azerbaijan). He brought the iron gate of the defeated city to Georgia and donated it to Gelati Monastery at Kutaisi, western Georgia. Despite this br
2 CONC illiant victory, Demetrius could hold Ganja only for a few years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1130, Demetrius revealed a plot of nobles, probably involving the king's half-brother Vakhtang. The King arrested the conspirators and executed one of their leaders, Ioanne Abuletisdze, in 1138 (or 1145). In 1154 David, Demetri
2 CONC us's elder son forced his father to abdicate and become a monk, receiving the monastic name Damian (Damianus). However, David died six months later and King Demetrius was restored to the throne. David was survived by his son Demna
2 CONC  who was regarded by the aristocratic opposition as a lawful pretender.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Demetrius was not as successful as his father David the Builder, Georgia remained a strong feudal power with a well-organized military and political system and a developed cultural and economical life.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1156 and was buried at Gelati Monastery.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is regarded as a saint in the Orthodox Church and his feast day is celebrated on May 23 on the Eastern Orthodox liturgical calendar.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of Demetrius's wife is unknown, but he had several children:
2 CONT
2 CONT David
2 CONT
2 CONT Rusudan, married firstly with sultan Masud Temirek and secondly with Sinjar Shah of Seljuk
2 CONT
2 CONT Giorgi, who succeeded him
2 CONT
2 CONT Bagrationi, who married prince of Kiev
