1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Farrukh-Zad was mentioned on page 129 of [https://books.google.com/books?id=BAN6ONlDkgIC&printsec=frontcover&source=gbs_ge_summary_r&cad=0#v=onepage&q&f=false '''The Annals of the Saljuq Turks: Selections from Al
2 CONC -Kamil Fi'l-Ta'rikh of Ibn Al-Athir'''], translated by D.S. Richards. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This year, during Safar (19 March-16 April 1059), King Farrukh-Zad ibn Mas'ud ibn Mahmud ibn Sabuktegin, lord of Ghazna, died. His mamlukes had rebelled against him during 450 (1058) and plotted to kill him. They attacked him whil
2 CONC e he was in the bath. He had a sword with him, which he took up and fought them with. He defended himself until his men came to his aid and rescued him, killing those mamlukes.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his escape from this incident, he spoke much of death and scorned and despised this present life. He continued in that vein until this year, when he was afflicted with an intestinal obstruction from which he died. He was suc
2 CONC ceeded by his brother, Ibrahim ibn Mas'ud, who ruled well. He made his preparations for jihad in India, and conquered fortresses which had resisted his father and his grandfather. He used to fast during Rajab, Sha'ban and Ramadan.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Farrukh-Zad_of_Ghazna '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Farrukh-Zad''' (Full name: ''Jamal ad-Dawlah Abu Shuja Farrukh-Zad''), was sultan of the Ghaznavid Empire from ca. 1053 to 4 April 1059. His reign was considered one of benevolence, prosperity and tranquility for the Ghaznavid 
2 CONC empire.[2] It was free of the chaotic turbulence and greed from palace ghulams until the end of his reign. He was very devout Muslim and fasted during Rajab, Sha'ban and Ramadan.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT Having been at the fortress of Barghund, Farrukh-Zad was one of the Ghaznavid princes that escaped the usurper Toghrul's massacre in 1052.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Farrukh employed Abd al-Razzaq Maymandi, a former vizier of Maudud and Abd Rashid, but later had him dismissed and imprisoned.[5] He also freed Abul-Fazl Bayhaqi from his imprisonment and it was during Farrukh's reign that Bayhaqi
2 CONC  wrote his Mujalladat (Tarikh-e Beyhaqi).[6][7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the chaos of Toghrul's usurpation, Chagri Beg sent an army to take Ghazni. However, the ghulam general Khirghiz intercept and defeated this Seljuq force.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1058, Farrukh had his army invade Tukharistan in hopes of removing the Seljuqs. His army was initially successful, capturing the Seljuq Atabeg Qutb ad-Din Kul-Sarigh. However, Alp Arslan counter-attacked and defeated the Gh
2 CONC aznavid army capturing many of its commanders. A subsequent peace treaty, draw up by Abul-Fazl Bayhaqi allowed for an exchange of prisoners and a mutual non-aggression pact.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Death=
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1058, palace ghulams attempted to assassinate Farrukh in his bath, but he grabbed a sword and held them at bay until his guards arrived and killed the ghulams.[9] Depressed and sickened by the attempt on his life, Farrukh-Zad w
2 CONC ithdrew from worldly affairs and died of colitis on 4 April 1059 at the age of thirty four.[10] He succeeded by his brother Ibrahim.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # "FARROḴZĀD, ABŪ ŠOJĀʿ" Encyclopædia Iranica
2 CONT # C.E. Bosworth, The Later Ghaznavids, 47.
2 CONT # 'Izz al-D in Ibn al-Athir, The Annals of the Saljuq Turks, transl. D.S. Richards, (Routledge, 2002), 129.
2 CONT # C.E. Bosworth, The Later Ghaznavids, (Columbia University Press, 1977), 46.
2 CONT # C.E. Bosworth, The Later Ghaznavids, 48.
2 CONT # Abu'l Fazl Bayhaqi, The A to Z of the United Nations, (Scarecrow Press, Inc., 2009), 59.
2 CONT # C.E. Bosworth, The Later Ghaznavids, 48.
2 CONT # C.E. Bosworth, The Later Ghaznavids, 49.
2 CONT # 'Izz al-D in Ibn al-Athir, The Annals of the Saljuq Turks, 129.
2 CONT # C.E. Bosworth, The Later Ghaznavids, 49.
2 CONT
