1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maw%27dud_of_Ghazni '''Wikipedia''']
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Shahāb-ud-Dawla Mawdūd''' (Persian: شهاب‌الدوله مودود‎; died 1050), known as Mawdud of Ghazni (مودود غزنوی), was sultan of the Ghaznavid Empire from 1041 to 1050. He seized the throne of the Ghaznavid Empire from his uncle Muha
2 CONC mmad of Ghazni in revenge for the murder of his father, Ma'sud I. His brother Majdud in Lahore did not recognize him as sultan, but his sudden death paved the way for Maw'dud to exercise control over the eastern portion of the Gha
2 CONC znavid Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maw'dud inherited an empire whose entire western half was overrun by the Seljuq Turks and was battling to continue existing. During his reign the further reaches of the Indian conquests and vassal states also broke away. Maw'dud w
2 CONC as able to hold on to his Afghan realms and Indus valley territories while stabilizing while pushing north into Central Asia and stabilizing his western front with the Seljuqs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Keikavus, author of the Qabus nama, was a guest at Maw'dud's court for seven to eight years.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Early life==
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1038, Maw'dud was declared by his father as the heir of the Empire. Furthermore, Maw'dud helped his father during his campaigns against the Seljuq Turks and the Karakhanids. However, Mas'ud was eventually defeated by the Seljuq
2 CONC s at Dandanaqan in 1040, and chose to leave Khorasan for India, but was taken captive by his own soldiers, and replaced with his brother Muhammad, who had him killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Reign==
2 CONT
2 CONT Maw'dud, who was at Balkh during that time with his father's vizier Ahmad Shirazi, then invaded the domains of Muhammad, and then avenged his father by defeating and killing him at Jalalabad in 1041. Maw'dud, now with the control 
2 CONC of all of the Ghaznavid Empire except Lahore, which was under the control of his rebellious brother Majdud, then appointed Ahmad Shirazi as his vizier, while Abu Sahl Zawzani was appointed as his chief secretary. In 1042, Maw'dud 
2 CONC invaded the territories of the Seljuqs and briefly occupied Balkh and Herat. This greatly increased the fame of Maw'dud and made the Karakhanid ruler Böritigin acknowledge him as his suzerain.[2] In 1043, Ahmad Shirazi fell out of
2 CONC  favor and was replaced with Abd al-Razzaq Maymandi as Maw'dud's vizier. During the same time, a rebellion in Sistan was quelled by Maw'dud's military slave Toghrul.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1043/4, Maw'dud invaded Tukharistan but was repelled by the Seljuq prince Alp Arslan. Furthermore, Maw'dud also sent soldiers to Sistan in order to hold his authority of the ruler of the region, the Nasrid ruler Abu'l-Fadl Nasr
2 CONC . However, these actions were fruitless, and Sistan soon became the vassals of the Seljuqs, thus the Ghaznavid borders were limited to Bost.[4] During the same time, Majdud died, and Maw'dud used the opportunity to capture Lahore.
2 CONC  However, a combined army of three Hindu princes, who had captured many cities from the Ghaznavids then besieged Lahore, but were defeated. Maw'dud then invaded Multan and repelled the Ismailis who lived in the region.
2 CONT
2 CONT In ca. 1050, Maw'dud, with the aid Böritigin and an army sent by the former Kakuyid ruler Garshasp I re-invaded Khorasan; Böritigin and his commander Qashgha invaded Khwarazm and Tirmidh, however, during the invasion, Maw'dud died
2 CONC  and thus the invasion failed. The Seljuqs then extended their rule as far as Vakhsh and appointed a certain Abu 'Ali ibn Shadhan as the governor of their new conquests.[5] After this, Böritigin seems to have stopped recognizing t
2 CONC he Ghaznavids as his suzerain. Maw'dud was succeeded by his son Mas'ud II.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # C.E. Bosworth, The Later Ghaznavids, 35.
2 CONT # Bosworth 1968, p. 52.
2 CONT # C.E. Bosworth, The Later Ghaznavids, 29.
2 CONT # Bosworth.
2 CONT # Bosworth 1968, p. 53.
2 CONT
