1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Ashot II, King of Armenia==
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikiwand.com/en/Ashot_II_of_Armenia
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Smbat I "the Martyr" King of Armenia and Rhipsime de Smbat I Bagratuni) Багратион 
2 CONT * Full name: Ashot II Bagratuni
2 CONT * Reign, 914-929
2 CONT * Born: 881-84
2 CONT * Died: 929
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Spouse==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Ashot was married to Marie, daughter of Prince Sahak, founder of the Kingdom of Artsakh.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Wikipedia==
2 CONT
2 CONT Ashot II (Armenian: Աշոտ Բ; r. 914–929) was an Armenian monarch and the third king of the royal Bagratuni line. He was the son and successor of King Smbat I. His reign was filled with rebellions by pretenders to the throne, and fo
2 CONC reign invasions, which Ashot fought off successfully, for which he is remembered by the epithet Yerkat (Երկաթ), or the Iron.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ashot II had succeeded his father Smbat I upon the latter's death in 914. Smbat had fought off an invasion launched by the emir of Azerbaijan (Iran), Yusuf Ibn Abi'l-Saj, but when Smbat surrendered he was tortured and beheaded by 
2 CONC Yusuf in Yernjak. With his control of the central lands of Armenia, Yusuf installed Ashot, son of Shapuh and Ashot II's first cousin, in Dvin as the "anti-king" of Bagratid Armenia.[2] Harried by Yusuf's forces, Ashot turned west 
2 CONC for support. In 914, he visited Constantinople to receive aid from the crown regent for the young Emperor Constantine Porphyrogenitus, Zoe Karbonopsina.
2 CONT
2 CONT There, Ashot was well received, and a Byzantine force was created to assist Armenia in defeating the Arabs. The force, accompanying Ashot and led by the Domestic of the Schools Leo Phokas, moved out the next year and marched along
2 CONC  Upper Euphrates, entering Taron, meeting scant opposition from the Arabs.[4] Ashot the anti-king and Yusuf's armies were unable to stop the Byzantine advance, which stopped short of capturing Dvin due to the onset of winter. Neve
2 CONC rtheless, the force had returned Ashot to a powerful position in Armenia and managed to inflict heavy casualties against the Arabs.
2 CONT
2 CONT This still left Ashot the anti-king in control of Dvin and civil war raged on from 918 to 920, when the pretender finally conceded defeat. Numerous other rebellions in Armenia also took place but Ashot was able to defeat each one 
2 CONC of them. In 919, Yusuf had instigated a failed rebellion against the Caliph and was replaced by a far more well-disposed Arab governor, Subuk. In 922 he was recognized as the ruler of Armenia by the Abbasid caliph in Baghdad and S
2 CONC ubuk recognized him as Shahanshah, or "king of kings."
2 CONT
2 CONT Ironically, the Byzantines were distressed with Ashot's close relations with the Arabs and dispatched a new force under the Domestic of the Schools John Kourkouas, also of Armenian descent, to disrupt Ashot's position as king and 
2 CONC to support the rebels fighting him. In 928, Kourkouas reached Dvin in an unsuccessful attempt to capture a city that was defended by both the Arabs and Ashot. In 923, the Caliph, facing troubles at home, released Yusuf, who travel
2 CONC ed back to Armenia to unleash his fury against Ashot.[7] He began demanding tribute from the Armenians rulers but faced considerable resistance by Ashot II. Time and again, Ashot was able to defeat and rout the Arab armies sent ag
2 CONC ainst him for several years. Finally, in 929, Yusuf died and a power struggle ensued between rival Iranian and Kurdish families in Āzarbāijān, thus reducing the Arab threat to Armenia. Byzantine emperor Romanos Lekapenos also turn
2 CONC ed his attention from the east to fight the Arabs in Syria. Ashot died without any sons or heirs. He was succeeded ca. 929 by his brother Abas.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source''' Wikipedia - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashot_II_of_Armenia
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Sources==
2 CONT * https://www.geni.com/people/Ripsimia/6000000002187821458 is NOT a known child 
