1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diya%27_al-Dawla '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Abu Tahir Firuzshah''' (Persian: ابو طاهر فیروز شاه‎), better known by his laqab of '''Diya' al-Dawla''', was the Buyid ruler of Basra during the 980s. He was the son of 'Adud al-Dawla.
2 CONT
2 CONT =History=
2 CONT
2 CONT Abu Tahir Firuzshah was the son of Adud al-Dawla and a daughter of Manadhar, who was an Justanid king. Following 'Adud al-Dawla's death in 983, his possessions were divided between his sons. Samsam al-Dawla, who was the presumed s
2 CONC uccessor of 'Adud al-Dawla, took power, but Sharaf al-Dawla took advantage of his position in Kerman to invade Fars. This invasion distracted Samsam al-Dawla and gave Abu Tahir Firuzshah the ability to set up his own independent r
2 CONC ule in Basra, where he took the title of Diya' al-Dawla.
2 CONT
2 CONT Diya' al-Dawla, as well as another brother, Taj al-Dawla, who controlled Khuzestan, eventually decided acknowledge the authority of Fakhr al-Dawla, who ruled in Jibal. This was done in an attempt to protect themselves from the con
2 CONC flict between Samsam al-Dawla and Sharaf al-Dawla; Basra and Khuzestan were situated in between the possessions of the two and were therefore vulnerable. Nevertheless, after a few years Sharaf al-Dawla invaded and occupied Basra a
2 CONC nd Khuzestan, causing the two princes to flee to Fakhr al-Dawla's territory. There they found refuge in Ray. Neither of the two brothers managed to found any lasting line; consequently their role in Buyid politics was short.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Bosworth, C. E. (1975). "Iran under the Buyids". In Frye, R. N. The Cambridge History of Iran, Volume 4: From the Arab Invasion to the Saljuqs. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press. pp. 250–305. ISBN 0-521-20093-8.
