1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sabuktigin '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Abu Mansur Sabuktigin''' (Persian: ابو منصور سبکتگین‎) (ca 942 – August 997), also spelled as ''Sabuktagin'', ''Sabuktakin'', ''Sebüktegin'' and ''Sebük Tigin'', was the founder of the Ghaznavid dynasty, ruling from 977 to 997.
2 CONC [3] In Turkic the name means beloved prince.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sabuktigin lived as a slave during his youth and later married the daughter of his master Alptigin, the man who seized the region of Ghazna (modern Ghazni Province in Afghanistan) in a political fallout for the throne of the Saman
2 CONC ids of Bukhara.[5] Although the latter and Sabuktigin still recognized Samanid authority, and it was not until the reign of Sabuktigin's son Mahmud that the rulers of Ghazni became independent.[3][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT When his father-in-law Alptigin died, Sebuktigin became the new ruler and expanded the kingdom after defeating Jayapala to cover the territory as far as the Neelum River in Kashmir and the Indus River in what is now Pakistan.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Early years
2 CONT * 2 Military career
2 CONT * 3 Censuring the people of innovation
2 CONT * 4 Death and legacy
2 CONT * 5 References
2 CONT * 6 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT =Early years=
2 CONT
2 CONT Sebuktigin was born around 942 CE in what is today Barskon, in Kyrgyzstan.[2] Around the age of twelve, Sebuktigin was taken prisoner by a neighbouring warring tribe and sold as a slave to a merchant named Nasr the Haji. He was ev
2 CONC entually purchased by Alptigin, the chamberlain of the Samanids of Bukhara.
2 CONT
2 CONT >"A merchant of the name of Nusr-Hajy having purchased Sabuktigin while yet a boy, brought him from the Turkic steppes to Bukhara, where he was sold to Aluptugeen, who, perceiving in him the promise of future greatness, raised him
2 CONC  by degrees to posts of confidence and distinction, till, at length, on his establishing his independence at Ghazni, he conferred on him the title of amir al-umara (chief of the nobles), and also that of Vakil-i-Mutluk, or Represe
2 CONC ntative."[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT —Minhaj al-Siraj Juzjani
2 CONT
2 CONT When Alptigin later rebelled against the Samanid rule, capturing Zabulistan and Ghazna south of the Hindu Kush in modern-day Afghanistan, he raised Sebuktigin to the position of a general and gave his daughter in marriage to him. 
2 CONC Subuktigin served Alptigin, and his two successors Ishaq and Balkatigin. He later succeeded another slave of Alptigin to the throne, and in 977 became the popular ruler of the Ghazna region south of the Hindu Kush.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sebuktigin enlarged upon Alptigin's conquests, extending his domain from Ghazna to Balkh in the north, Helmand in the west, and the Indus River in what is today Pakistan.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sebuktigin was recognized by the Caliph in Baghdad as governor of his dominions. He died in 997, and was succeeded by his younger son Ismail of Ghazni. Sebuktigin's older son, Mahmud, rebelled against his younger brother and took 
2 CONC over Ghazna as the new emir.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferishta records Sebuktigin's genealogy as descended from the Sassanid emperors: "Sabuktigin, the son of Jukan, the son of Kuzil-Hukum, the son of Kuzil-Arslan, the son of Firuz, the son of Yezdijird, king of Persia."[8] Some doub
2 CONC t has been cast on this due the lineage having been reckoned as too short to account for the 320 intervening years.[citation needed] What is known about Sebuktigin is that he was of Turkic origin.[2] According to Grousset,
2 CONT
2 CONT >"The Turkic mercenary army which Alptigin had raised in Ghazni, and which was already profoundly influenced by Islam, was from 977 onward led by another Turkic ex-slave -another Mameluke- named Sebuktigin, who made himself master
2 CONC  of Tokharistan (Balkh-Kunduz) and Kandahar, and embarked upon the conquest of Kabul."[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Military career=
2 CONT
2 CONT Sebuktegin grew up in the court circles of Alptigin and was conferred the titles of Amīr al-umara (Chief of the Nobles), and Wakīl-e Mūtlak (Representative), ultimately being made general. He was then heavily involved in the defen
2 CONC ce of Ghazna's independence for the next 15 years, until Alptigin's death in 975.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon Alptigin's death, both Sebuktegin and Alptigin's son Abu Ishaq went to Bukhara to mend fences with the Samanids. Mansur I then officially conferred upon Abu Ishaq the governorship of Ghazna and acknowledged Sebuktegin as the 
2 CONC heir. Abu Ishaq died soon after in 977 and Sabuktigin succeeded him in the governorship of Ghazna; subsequently marrying Alptigin's daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 977 he marched against Toghan, who had opposed his succession. Toghan fled to Bost, so Sebuktigin marched upon it and captured Kandahar and its surrounding area. This prompted the Shahi King Jayapala to launch an attack on Ghaz
2 CONC na. Despite the fact that Jayapala amassed about 100,000 troops for the battle, Sebuktigin was soundly victorious.[10] The battle was fought at Laghman (near Kabul) and Jayapala was forced to pay a large tribute. He defaulted upon
2 CONC  the payments, imprisoned Sebuktigin's collectors, and assembled a more larger army of 100,000 horse and an innumerable host of foot, allied with forces from the kingdoms of Delhi, Ajmer, Kalinjar, and Kannauj, which was defeated 
2 CONC in battle with Sebuktigin's Ghaznavids at the banks of the Neelum River in Kashmir.[8] Sebuktegin then annexed the regions of Afghanistan, Peshawar, and all the lands west of the Neelum River.
2 CONT
2 CONT >"The Afghans and Khiljies who resided among the mountains having taken the oath of allegiance to Sabuktigin, many of them were enlisted in his army, after which he returned in triumph to Ghazni."[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 994 he was involved in aiding Nuh II of the Samanids against internal uprisings and defeated the rebels at Balkh and then at Nishapur, thereby earning for himself the title of Nāsir ud-Dīn ("Hero of the Faith") and for his son 
2 CONC Mahmud the title of Governor of Khorasan and Saif ud-Dawlah ("Sword of the State").[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sebuktigin had increased upon Alptigin's domains by extending his domain to cover the area south of the Hindu Kush in Afghanistan and east to the Indus River in what is today Pakistan; he was eventually recognized by the Caliph in
2 CONC  Baghdad as governor of his dominions.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Censuring the people of innovation=
2 CONT
2 CONT A pious ruler, Sebuktegin grew concerned over the increasing amount of innovation (commonly known as bidah) in the Islamic creed, and consequently censured those who he believed were promulgating heretical doctrines or beliefs tha
2 CONC t contravened orthodox Sunni principles.[12] Ibn Taymiyyah duly eulogizes Sebüktegin, stating that he:
2 CONT
2 CONT >"commanded that Ahlul Bidah be publicly cursed on the minbars, and as a result the Jahmiyyah, Rafida, Hululiyah, Mu'tazilah, and Qadariyah were all publicly cursed, along with the Asharites."[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Death and legacy=
2 CONT
2 CONT After becoming sick during one of his campaigns, Sebuktigin died in August 997 while travelling from Balkh to Ghazni in Afghanistan. The nature of his illness is unknown and the exact location of his death is uncertain. Minhaj al-
2 CONC Siraj Juzjani, a 13th-century historian, stated that "Sabuktigin died in the village of (Bermel Madwari, or Madar wa Moi, or Madawri, or Madraiwi, or Barmel Maderwi)." In modern times, Henry George Raverty has also mentioned Terme
2 CONC z in his translations of the village name. Firishta, a 16th-century historian, has also mentioned Termez as the place of death of Subuktageen.[14] Abdul Hai Habibi believes that Sebuktigin's place of death is Marmal, Mazar-i-Shari
2 CONC f.[15] He was buried in a tomb in Ghazni which can be visited by tourists. He was succeeded by his younger son, Ismail. Sebuktegin is generally regarded as the architect of the Ghaznavid Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sabuktigin#References '''1-15''']
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sabuktigin#Sources '''1-3''']
