1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghaznavids#Family_tree_of_the_Ghaznavid_sultans '''Hurra-yi Kalji'''], Princess, married Mamunid ruler Ma'mun II.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khwarazmian_dynasty '''Wikipedia page'''] of the Khwarazmian dynasty:
2 CONT
2 CONT The date of the founding of the Khwarazmian dynasty remains debatable. During a revolt in 1017, Khwarezmian rebels murdered Abu'l-Abbas Ma'mun and his wife, Hurra-ji, sister of the Ghaznavid sultan Mahmud.[15] In response, Mahmud 
2 CONC invaded and occupied the region of Khwarezm, which included Nasa and the ribat of Farawa.[16] As a result, Khwarezm became a province of the Ghaznavid Empire from 1017 to 1034.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [15] C.E. Bosworth, The Ghaznavids:994-1040, (Edinburgh University Press, 1963), 237.
2 CONT * [16] C.E. Bosworth, The Ghaznavids:994-1040, 237.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ma%27mun_II '''Wikipedia page'''] of her husband, Ma'mun II:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ma'mun also married Mahmud's sister Hurra-yi Kalji, who had previously been married to his brother, in 1015 or 1016.[1].
2 CONT
2 CONT '''NOTE:''' The Wikipedia page does not state whether Hurra-yi Kalij was married to Ma'mun II's brother Abu al-Hasan Ali or to another brother, but it is likely that the brother in question was Abu al-Hasan Ali.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [1] '' The Early Ghaznavids'', C.E. Bosworth, The Cambridge History of Iran, Vol.4, ed. R. N. Frye, (Cambridge University Press, 1999), 174.
