1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Shen Zhong 沈眾 (503-558), ''zi'' Zhongshi 仲師. Liang and Chen period literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shen Zhong's ancestral home was Wukang 武康 in Wuxing 吳興 (modern Deqing 德清, Huzhou 湖州, Zhejiang). His grandfather was the celebrated writer Shen Yue 沈約 (441-513) of the Liang period. His father was Shen Xuan 沈旋 (fl. 480-513) who ser
2 CONC ved in various official positions during the early Liang.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his youth Shen Zhong was a diligent student and skilled writer. His first official appointment was administrator in the Law Section in the household of Xiao Wei 蕭偉 (476-553), Prince of Nanping 南平. He then served as secretary to
2 CONC  the heir designate Xiao Gang 蕭綱 (503-551). At that time, Emperor Wu (r. 502-549) of the Liang dynasty composed "Qianzi shi" 千字詩 (A thousand-charater poem). He requested Shen Zhong to write a commentary to it. Emperor Wu also summ
2 CONC oned him and Xie Jing 謝景 (n.d.) to the Wende 文德 Hall. He ordered Shen Zhong to composed "Zhu fu" 竹賦 (''Fu'' on the bamboo). When the piece was completed, Emperor Wu was quite pleased. He remarked:" Your literary style is elegant, 
2 CONC and you did not disgrace yoru grandfather" (''Chen shu'' 12.243).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ca. 535, Xiao Daxin 蕭大心 (523-551), Duke of Dangyang 當陽, was serving as regional inspector of Yingzhou 郢州 (administrative headquarters Runan 汝南, modern Wuchang 武昌, Hubei). He recruited Shen Zhong to serve as his secretarial aide. I
2 CONC n 540, he was assigned as secretarial aide to Xiao Yi 蕭繹 (508-555), Prince of Xiangdong 湘東. He later was promoted to the position of secretary on the staff of the heir designate Xiao Gang, and concurrently served as cavalier atten
2 CONC dant-in-ordinary. After he returned from making a brief diplomatic visit to the Northern Wei court, he was transferred to the post of administrative adviser to Xiao Xu 蕭續 (504-547), Prince of Luling 盧陵. He also continued to serve 
2 CONC as secretary to the heir designate Xiao Gang.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Hou Jing 侯景 Rebellion erupted in 549, Shen Zhong proposed to Emperor Wu to be allowed to return to his home area of Wuxing and recruit soldiers. The emperor granted his request. When Hou Jing's army surrounded the capital
2 CONC  area, Shen Zhong led an army of more than five thousand of his clansmen and volunteers that he recruited to defend the Liang house. Shen Zhong's armed forces strongly held their lines, and even Hou Jing feared them. When Hou Jing
2 CONC  captured the capital, Shen Zhong surrendered to Hou Jing.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Hou Jing Rebellion was put down, Shen Zhong went westward to Jingzhou 荊州 (administrative headquaters Jiangling 江陵, modern Jingzhou city, Hebei) where he joined court of Xiao Yi who was installed as emperor in December 55
2 CONC 2. Shen Zhong was appointed palace cadet in the household of the heir designate and grand rectifier of Jingzhou. Within a short time, he was promoted to the post of left administrator in the Ministry of Education.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shen Zhong was captured when Jiangling fell to the Western Wei in 555. He escaped and returned to Jiankang. Xiao Fangzhi 蕭方智 (543-558), Emperor Jing (r. 555-557) appointed him palace attendant, and later assigned him as minister i
2 CONC n the census section of the Department of State Affairs. When the Liang fell in 557, Chen Baxian 陳霸先 (503-559), Emperor Wu (r. 557-559), established the Chen dynasty. Shen Zhong joined the Chen court and was appointed secretarial 
2 CONC director. He continued to serve as grand rectifier for his home commandery of Jingzhou. Emperor Wu treated him with more respect than his peers because of his fine reputation. In 558, he was assigned to the position of minister in
2 CONC  the Ministry of Public Constructions in which capacity he oversaw the construction of the Taiji 太極 Hall. Even though Shen Zhong was an extremely wealthy person -- his wealth could be counted in the hundreds of millions of cash --
2 CONC  he was extremely parsimonious, and he maintained an extremely frugal life style. When other court officials criticized him for his stinginess, Shen Zhong began to malign them. When Emperor Wu learned of Shen's conduct, he was out
2 CONC raged. Unwilling to punish him openly, he waited until Shen Zhong went home to Wukang on leave. He then ordered Shen to commit suicide. He was fifty-six at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shen Zhong's best known literary piece is "''Fu'' on the bamboo" mentioned above. It is no longer extant.
2 CONT
2 CONT Taiping Chang
2 CONT
2 CONT Citation
2 CONT * 沈眾 Shen Zhong, Brill’s Chinese Reference Shelf. Brill Online, 2017. http://chinesereferenceshelf.brillonline.com/ancient-literature/entries/COM-0039
