1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ma%27mun_II '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Abu'l-Abbas Ma'mun ibn Ma'mun''' (died March 1017) was the Ma'munid ruler of Khwarazm from 1008 or 1009 until his death, having succeeded his brother Abu al-Hasan Ali in that post. He was the son of Ma'mun I ibn Muhammad.
2 CONT
2 CONT The greatest threat to Ma'mun's rule came in the form of the Ghaznavid sultan, Mahmud of Ghazni. Mahmud viewed Khwarazm as a strategically important province, as it would allow him to widen the front against his biggest enemy, the
2 CONC  Karakhanids of Transoxiana. When the caliph al-Qadir sent Ma'mun several awards, including an investiture patent for Khwarazm (confirming him as independent ruler) in 1014, Ma'mun refused to accept the awards in his capital, fear
2 CONC ing that personally accepting the symbols of independence would anger Mahmud. He instead sent out a delegation to accept the awards on the steppe. Ma'mun also married Mahmud's sister Hurra-yi Kalji, who had previously been married
2 CONC  to his brother, in 1015 or 1016.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite these efforts to placate Mahmud, the Ghaznavid demanded that Ma'mun place his name in the khutba, in effect acknowledging his suzerainty. Although the nobility and the army were opposed to such measure, Ma'mun had no choic
2 CONC e but to give in. He agreed to place Mahmud's name in the khutba and to fulfil other humiliating demands. In response the army revolted and Ma'mun was killed. The rebels placed his nephew Muhammad on the throne, but Mahmud used th
2 CONC e death of his brother-in-law as a pretext for annexing Khwarazm.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT During Ma'mun's reign several scholars, such as al-Biruni, resided in Khwarazm, and in fact one of Mahmud's demands upon the shah was that several of them be sent to him. Ma'mun also was responsible for several building projects; 
2 CONC a minaret that he ordered to be constructed still stands in Konya-Urgench.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # The Early Ghaznavids, C.E. Bosworth, The Cambridge History of Iran, Vol.4, ed. R. N. Frye, (Cambridge University Press, 1999), 174.
2 CONT # The Early Ghaznavids, C.E. Bosworth, The Cambridge History of Iran, Vol.4, 174.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Bosworth, C. Edmund (1984). "ĀL-E MAʾMŪN". Encyclopaedia Iranica, Vol. I, Fasc. 7. C. Edmund Bosworth. pp. 762–764.
