2 CONT "Abu Ishaq was born on September 21, 953,[1] and was the grandson of the Dailamite officer Ispahdost on his mother's side. Abu Ishaq had 4 brothers named Bakhtiyar, Habashi, Marzuban, Abu Tahir, and a sister named Zubayda. Abu Ish
2 CONC aq, during his youth, was tutored in Baghdad.[2]..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Abu Ishaq, along with his mother whose name is unknown, the Abbasid Caliph al-Muti, including the women and children of the city, fortified themselves in a castle, waiting for Sabuktakin to return to Baghdad.[4] The vizier of 'Iz
2 CONC z al-Dawla, Ibn Baqiyya, shortly arrived to Baghdad and aided Abu Ishaq in strengthening the defenses of Baghdad.[4] In the end a treaty was made, which resulted in the restoration of Mosul and its surrounding areas to the Hamdani
2 CONC ds.[5]..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Some months later, 'Izz al-Dawla, who had financial difficulties, tried to solve them by seizing the Turkish fiefs, most of which were in Khuzestan. At the same time, he dismissed Sabuktakin from his post. These actions made most
2 CONC  of the army become hostile to 'Izz al-Dawla, and while 'Izz al-Dawla was away from Baghdad, the army under Sabuktakin then wished to make Abu Ishaq to become the new Buyid ruler of Iraq. Abu Ishaq shortly received these news, and
2 CONC  at first thought to join them, but after the urging from his mother, he declined the proposal.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT The army then shortly openly rebelled, and Abu Ishaq along with his mother and brother Abu Tahir, including their followers, then made an agreement with Sabuktakin to stop resistance in return for safety.[7] The rebels shortly man
2 CONC aged to capture Baghdad, and then marched towards Wasit, where 'Izz al-Dawla had forfitred himself. During the siege, Sabuktakin died, and was succeeded by Alptakin as the leader of the rebels."
2 CONT
