1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT 1107
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cumans
2 CONT
2 CONT Sharukan/Sharagan (also known as Sharukan the Elder), grand father of Konchak. He was another Polovotsian khan who was victorious against the Ruthenian army of Yaroslavichi at the Alta river (Battle of the Alta River). According t
2 CONC o the Novgorod First Chronicle Sharukan was taken as prisoner by Svyatoslav II of Kiev in 1068, while no such information is provided in the Laurentian Codex. In May 1107 along with Bonyak, Sharukan raided a couple of Ruthenian ci
2 CONC ties (Pereyaslav and Lubny), however already in August of the same year the collective Ruthenian army led by Svyatoslav carried out a devastating defeat to the Cuman Horde forcing Sharukan to flee
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sharokan/Sharohan/Sharukan, (Cuman-Kipchak base name Sharu/Sharo), chieftain, Konchak's grandfather, who had been defeated by the Russians in a great battle on the Sula River in 1107. Called "the Old" by Russians.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_people_of_Cuman_descent
2 CONT
2 CONT Also see ''The Romanians and the Turkic Nomads North of the Danube Delta''  https://books.google.com/books?isbn=9004175369 by Victor Spinei - 2009 
2 CONT
2 CONT and
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Encyclopedia of Ukraine: Volume III: L-Pf'' - Google Books Result https://books.google.com/books?isbn=1442651253 Danylo Husar Struk - 1993
2 CONT
2 CONT =-------------------------------=
2 CONT Sharukan’ (from http://encyclopedia2.thefreedictionary.com/Sharukan), a city of the 11th to early 13th centuries apparently located on the Severskii Donets near the cities of Chuguev and Zmiev. Sharukhan’, whose name derives from 
2 CONC that of the '''Polovtsian khan Sharukan''', arose as an Alani settlement. The city had an ethnically mixed population and served as the winter refuge of the Polovtsy. In 1111 it was captured by troops under the command of Vladimir
2 CONC  Vsevolodovich Monomakh and other Russian princes. During the 12th and early 13th centuries, Sharukan’ was gradually abandoned.
2 CONT
2 CONT REFERENCE Pletneva, S. A. “Polovetskaia zemlia.” In Drevnerusskie kniazhestva X–XIII vv. Moscow, 1975.
2 CONT
