1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The source behind Shushandukht’s historical narrative seems to be ''Šahrestānīhā ī Ērānšahr (The Provincial Capitals of Eranshahr)'' compiled in the 8th - 9th century in Persian, which mentions  a Jewish queen marr
2 CONC ied to Yazdegerd I. The relevant passage says:
2 CONT
2 CONT >''She is described as “daughter of the res-galutag (Exilarch)”, wife of Yazdegerd, and mother of Bahram/Gur.''
2 CONT * https://www.toviapreschel.com/shushandukht-another-jewish-persian-queen-900-years-after-queen-esther/?utm_source=chatgpt.com  
2 CONT
2 CONT Crucially, the text does not name the Exilarch - only that her father held that position .
2 CONT
2 CONT From the unnamed “daughter of the Exilarch” reference, James Darmesteter hypothesized that her father might have been '''[https://www.geni.com/people/Kahana-ben-Abba-Mari-23rd-Exilarch-Mar-Kahana-I/6000000000802879019 Kahana ben A
2 CONC bba Mari]''', based on manuscript timing and rabbinic genealogy 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Jewish Encyclopedia recognizes the authenticity of the Pahlavi passage but notes ambiguity regarding the Exilarch’s identity, proposing possibilities including '''[https://www.geni.com/people/Kahana-ben-Abba-Mari-23rd-Exilarch
2 CONC -Mar-Kahana-I/6000000000802879019?through=6000000003828265997 Mar Kahana], Mar Yemar, or [https://www.geni.com/people/Zutra-ben-Kahana-25th-Exilarch-Mar-Zutra-I/6000000003828265997 Mar Zutra I]''' - all in office circa 407 CE when
2 CONC  Bahram was born:
2 CONT
2 CONT It also '''argues against Huna bar Nathan as a candidate, asserting he never held the title of Exilarch'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >''Yezdegerd's Jewish Wife.
2 CONT >''The references to the Jews in the "Shatroiha-i Eran" allude to the Sassanian period, and concern chiefly the Jewish wife of Yezdegerd I. (399-420). According to §§ 46-47, "the cities of Shus [Shushan] and Shuster were built by 
2 CONC Shoshan-dukht [?], wife of Yazdkarṭ I., son of Shahpuhr [Sapor]. She was the daughter of the Reshgalutak, King of the Jews; she was the mother of Bahram [V.] Gor." The reading of the name of this princess is somewhat uncertain. It
2 CONC  can scarcely be "Shayan-dokht," as Darmesteter first thought; but his later conjecture that it is "Shasyandokht" is more plausible. A still better reading is "Shoshan-dukht," regarded by Blochet as formed from the Hebrew  ("lily"
2 CONC ) and the Pahlavi "dukht," "dokht" = "daughter" (for similar names having "dukht" as the last member, see Justi, l.c. pp. 492-493). The foundation of the two cities Shosh and Shoshtar by her is explained by Marquart ("Erānšahr," p
2 CONC p. 53 (note 1), 144, Berlin, 1901) as a tradition based on the similarity of their names to her own, although it may also denote that she established Jewish colonies in them. The name "Shoshan-dukht" is likewise read, however, "Ga
2 CONC syan"dukht" (= "glory of the throne") by Justi (ib. Supplement). The high station of the "resh galuta" or Exilarch in Persia at this period makes such a marriage between his daughter and the king not altogether improbable, while s
2 CONC uch epithets as "alathim," "al-khashm" (= "hard," "wicked"), "dafr" (= "stinking"), and "bazah-kar" (= "sinner"), applied to Yezdegerd, find their explanation in great part in his religious laxity as shown by his toleration of Jew
2 CONC s and Christians during a portion of his reign (comp. Justi in Geiger and Kuhn, l.c. ii. 526; Modi, "Aiyadgar-i Zariran," etc., pp. 137-143).''''
2 CONT
2 CONT >''The particular exilarch who was the father of Shoshan-dukht or Gasyan-dukht is not certain, although, from the passage in the "Shatroiha-i Eran," he would seem to have been in office in 407, the year in which Bahram Gur or Gor 
2 CONC was born (comp. Spiegel, l.c. iii. 341). He may have been, therefore, either Mar Kahana or Mar Yemar or Mar Zuṭra I., who successively filled the position of resh galuta for brief periods about that time (Grätz, "Gesch." 3d ed., i
2 CONC v. 463). At all events the queen can hardly have been the daughter of Huna b. Nathan, as has been supposed; for, despite the favor which he enjoyed at the court of Yezdegerd, he was never exilarch. According to the "Shatroiha-i Er
2 CONC an," moreover, the same Jewish princess established a colony of her coreligionists at Gai, a quarter of Ispahan (§ 54). In like manner Narses, "the son of a Jewess" ("Narsai Yahuṭakan"), "founded" the city of Khwarezm, the modern 
2 CONC Khiva (ib. § 10), although the town itself is mentioned as early as the Avesta ("Yasht," x. 14) under the name "Hvairizem" (comp. Geiger, "Ostiranische Kultur im Altertum," pp. 29-30, Erlangen, 1882; Modi translates, in his "Aiyad
2 CONC gar-i Zariran," etc., p. 61, "The chief of the Jews founded the city of Khvarzem"). This Narses was evidently the younger brother of Bahram Gur; he could scarcely have been the vizier of his father, Yezdegerd I. (comp. Justi, "Ira
2 CONC nisches Namenbuch," p. 223a).'' 
2 CONT * https://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/articles/11851-pahlavi-literature-jews-in?utm_source=chatgpt.com
2 CONT
2 CONT In  ''The Curious Case of the Jewish Sasanian Queen Šīšīnduxt:
2 CONT Exilarchal Propaganda and Zoroastrians in Tenth- to Eleventh-Century Baghdad'' (2021), Simcha Gross posits that  '''the narrative that identifies the Exilarch by name may be a later construction postdating the original text, servi
2 CONC ng to bolster later Exilarchal prestige''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT >''The Provincial Capitals of Ērānšahr, a medieval Zoroastrian Middle Persian text, recounts how the daughter of the Jewish exilarch married the Sasanian king Yaz- dgird I and gave birth to Wahrām Gōr, his successor. While the his
2 CONC toricity of the text has been largely undermined, scant attention has been given to its authorship and purpose. This article proposes that the story’s creators were members of the exilarch’s household in the tenth through eleventh
2 CONC  century who internalized the broader concern with (invented) Sasanian pedigree during the period known as the Iranian intermezzo in an effort to appeal to Iranian Jews and other elites alike''
2 CONT
2 CONT Gross points out that the Šahrestānīhā ī Ērānšahr  clearly states that Yazdegerd I married the daughter of the Exilarch, and that she was the mother of Bahram V, but does not name either the queen or her father.
2 CONT
2 CONT He examines how, over time, Jewish sources began to retroactively supply names, particularly Huna bar Nathan, to this otherwise anonymous figure. He writes that: “The historicization of this marriage and its association with known
2 CONC  rabbinic figures reflects a process of rabbinization or Judaization of Sasanian royal history…”
2 CONT
2 CONT So the later attribution of names like Huna bar Nathan is post-Pahlavi, appearing in medieval Jewish historiography and genealogy  was likely done to legitimize and elevate the prestige of the Exilarchate by associating it with Sa
2 CONC sanian royalty.
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://lockwoodonlinejournals.com/index.php/jaos/article/view/633
2 CONT * https://law-journals-books.vlex.com/vid/the-curious-case-of-870700845?utm_source=chatgpt.com
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shushandukht
