1 CONT Empress Wu and was made heir to the throne, his family name was
1 CONT changed into Wu which was the family name of his mother. 
1 CONT 2. His original family name Li, the family name of the emperors of
1 CONT the Tang Dynasty, was restored at a date not indicated by ancient
1 CONT Chinese historical books, perhaps in 705 at the fall of Empress Wu. 
1 CONT 3. This is the original given name of Emperor Ruizong. The literal
1 CONT meaning is "disk of the sun appearing above the horizon
1 CONT at daybreak". 
1 CONT 4. Had his name shortened into Lun ("disk") in December 669. 
1 CONT 5. Had his name changed into Dan (literal meaning: "dawn")
1 CONT in August 683. 
1 CONT 6. Had his name changed back to Lun when he ceded the imperial
1 CONT throne to his mother Empress Wu in October 690 and was made
1 CONT heir to the throne. 
1 CONT 7. Had his name changed back to Dan after he was reduced to a
1 CONT princely rank in 698 when his older brother Zhongzong replaced
1 CONT him as heir to the throne. This name became his taboo name when
1 CONT he was restored as emperor in 710. 
1 CONT 8. Ceded the imperial throne to his mother Empress Wu who
1 CONT became "emperor" while Ruizong was made heir to the throne. 
1 CONT 9. Abdicated in favor of his son Emperor Xuanzong and received
1 CONT the title Taishang Huang (???). In practice, continued to rule
1 CONT through his son until the palace coup of July 29, 713, and officially
1 CONT ceded full power to his son on July 30. 
1 CONT 10. Final version of his posthumous name as given in 754. 
1 CONT Emperor Ruizong (June 22, 662 – July 13, 716), personal name Li Dan, was the fifth and ninth emperor of Tang Dynasty. He was the eighth son of Emperor Gaozong and Empress Wu.
1 CONT
1 CONT In February 684, his mother Empress Wu demoted his older brother Emperor Zhongzong who had attempted to rule free of his mother, and named him emperor. Ruizong, however, was under control of his mother and didn't have any real pow
1 CONC er. In October 690 he ceded the imperial throne to his mother who was installed as "emperor" (huangdi, ??), the only woman in Chinese history ever to rule as emperor. Ruizong was reduced to the position of "heir to the throne". In
1 CONC  the following years, Empress Wu's family members (from the Wu family) tried to have one of them named heir to the throne, but the empress resisted these calls. Eventually, in October 698, faced with foreign invasion and dissatisf
1 CONC action at home, Empress Wu yielded to officials still loyal to the Li family (the Tang imperial family overthrown by the empress) and so she clearly hinted that she would be succeeded by a member of the Li family by appointing her
1 CONC  son the demoted Emperor Zhongzong as heir to the throne. On that occasion, Ruizong was reduced to a princely rank.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ruizong lived the next twelve years away from political intrigues and did not take part in the coup that saw the overthrow of Empress Wu and the restoration of Zhongzong as emperor in 705. The five years of Zhongzong's reign were 
1 CONC dominated by Zhongzong's empress consort, Empress Wei (???), and her lover Wu Sansi (???), the nephew of the deceased Empress Wu, who both dominated the weak emperor. In the beginning of July 710, Emperor Zhongzong died, allegedly
1 CONC  poisoned by Empress Wei who then named Zhongzong's only surviving son, a teenager called Li Chongmao, as emperor. A mere two weeks later, Princess Taiping, the older sister of Ruizong, launched a coup with the help of Ruizong's t
1 CONC hird son, Li Longji (the future Emperor Xuanzong), which resulted in the death of Empress Wei and the overthrow of the teenage emperor. Ruizong was unwillingly restored as emperor on July 25 by his sister and his son. Li Longji, w
1 CONC ho had proved his worth during the coup, was named crown prince with the consent of Ruizong's eldest son.
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon, however, tensions between Princess Taiping and Li Longji mounted. Princess Taiping, who had many supporters at court, hoped to ascend the imperial throne as her mother Empress Wu had done. Ruizong exiled his sister to the pr
1 CONC ovinces, hoping to defuse tension, but Li Longji, who feared that Princess Taiping would hold him responsible for her exile and would have him murdered, asked his father to recall the princess, who was allowed to return to the imp
1 CONC erial court. Eventually, in September 712, a disgruntled Ruizong, tired of court feuds, abdicated in favor of his son the crown prince Li Longji. Suddenly it was Princess Taiping who was at risk of falling victim to her nephew the
1 CONC  new emperor. The princess, alarmed by Ruizong's decision, managed to have the court name him Taishang Huang (???, sometimes translated as "retired emperor", or "emperor emeritus"), a position in which Ruizong was to keep effectiv
1 CONC e control of power with the new emperor Xuangzong only formally attending court audiences and ceremonies.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fearing for her future, Princess Taiping launched a coup in the end of July 713 to overthrow Emperor Xuanzong. As she was a woman and could not personally lead troops, the coup failed and all her supporters were killed. Princess T
1 CONC aiping fled to a Buddhist monastery in the mountains. She returned three days later and was allowed to commit suicide at home. On July 30, one day after the coup, Ruizong officially announced he was ceding full power to his son Em
1 CONC peror Xuanzong. Ruizong was allowed to keep the title Taishang Huang but lived away from politics until his death three years later in 716.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Emperor Zhongzong Emperor of Tang China
1 CONT 684–690 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Empress Wu 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Emperor Shang Emperor of Tang China
1 CONT 710–712 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Emperor Xuanzong 
0 @N6000000002188099930@ NOTE Princess Taiping
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Taiping (Chinese: ????) (between 651 and 662 – October 1 or 2, 713), was a princess of Tang dynasty of China. She was the daughter of Emperor Gaozong and Empress Wu Zetian. She had become an important and respected figure
1 CONC  during her mother's reign as Empress under the second Zhou dynasty 690-705.
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Taiping was an advocate for the preservation of the Tang dynasty and its imperial Li family. After the death of Empress Wu Zetian in 705, Princess Taiping aligned herself with her nephew Li Longji (later Emperor Xuanzong)
1 CONC  to regain power for the Tang dynasty. In 710, she and Li Longji successfully foiled Empress Wei's plot to gain control of power.
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Taiping was well-known for her beauty. In the Chinese 'black' history (mishi/??), it has been rumoured that Princess Taiping after failure of two past marriages, had a love affair with her nephew Li Long Ji (Xuan Zong). M
1 CONC ishi says that Xuan Zong actually confessed his love for his aunt in his autobiography. Li Long Ji confessed that Princess Taiping was his love at first sight. He pictured her as the divine serenity and she was all he could dream 
1 CONC of in perfection. The truth still remains hidden however.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Emperor Xuanzong ascended the throne in 712, Princess Taiping's seniority and power was seen as a threat to the young emperor and after a brief struggle, Princess Taiping was given an honorable death sentence to commit suici
1 CONC de by hanging herself in 713.
0 @N6000000002188099888@ NOTE Emperor Xuanzong of Tang
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT This article is about the well-known sixth emperor of Tang Dynasty. For his descendant whose temple name is also rendered Xuanzong in pinyin, see Emperor Xuanzong of Tang.
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Xuanzong of Tang 
1 CONT Birth and death: 8 September 685–May 3, 762 
1 CONT Family name: Li (?) 
1 CONT Given name: Longji (??) 
1 CONT Dates of reign: 8 September 712¹–12 August 756² 
1 CONT Temple name: Xuanzong (??) 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT
1 CONT (short) Emperor Ming³ (??) 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT
1 CONT (full) Emperor Zhidao Dasheng
1 CONT
1 CONT Daming Xiao
1 CONT ?????????
1 CONT
1 CONT General note: Dates given here are in the Julian calendar.
1 CONT
1 CONT They are not in the proleptic Gregorian calendar.
1 CONT ———
1 CONT 1. Ascended the throne following the abdication of his father, who
1 CONT received the title Taishang Huang (???).
1 CONT In practice, his father continued to rule until the palace coup
1 CONT of July 29, 713, and Xuanzong obtained full power from his
1 CONT father on July 30.
1 CONT 2. Following the rebellion of An Lushan, Xuanzong's son was
1 CONT proclaimed emperor by the army on August 12, 756, but
1 CONT Xuanzong and his retinue, who had escaped to Sichuan, only
1 CONT heard the news on September 10, 756, that date marking the
1 CONT end of Xuanzong's reign in practice.
1 CONT 3. This is the name under which Emperor Xuanzong is most
1 CONT known inside China.
1 CONT
1 CONT Emperor Tang Xuanzong (Chinese: ???) (September 8, 685 - May 3, 762), born Li Longji (???), was the sixth emperor of the Tang dynasty of China, reigning from 712 to 756.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Accession to the throne 
1 CONT 2 Kaiyuan era 
1 CONT 3 Later years 
1 CONT 4 Abdication and death 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Accession to the throne
1 CONT Born into an era when power was virtually in the hands of his grandmother, the Empress Wu Zetian, Xuanzong became the only glimmer of hope for the restoration of the imperial Li family. His aunt, the Princess Taiping fiercely prot
1 CONC ected Xuanzong from harm and was credited with taking care of young Xuanzong from the Wu family. In 710 Xuanzong conspired with Princess Taiping (daughter of Empress Wu Zetian) to put an end to Empress Wei's attempted usurpation o
1 CONC f power. He killed Empress Wei, the wife of his recently dead uncle Emperor Zhongzong, in a palace coup which placed his own father, Emperor Ruizong, on the throne. He was appointed as the Chancellor for a few months before he bec
1 CONC ame the crown prince. Xuanzong himself succeeded the throne in 712.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Kaiyuan era
1 CONT Known also as Tang Minghuang (???: "the understanding emperor of the Tang"), the early half of his reign (712-730's) saw Tang China reach the height of its powers, in a period known popularly as the Kaiyuan era (????). At the begi
1 CONC nning, Xuanzong was a hardworking and diligent emperor. He made sweeping reforms to the bureaucracy, employed capable ministers and also made contacts with foreign ambassadors as far west as the Middle East and greatly expanded Ch
1 CONC ina's borders. This period witnessed the nurturing of a cooperative relationship with the Unified Silla state on the Korean peninsula, then under the rule of King Seongdeok. Xuanzong saw in Silla a valuable ally on its flank, part
1 CONC icularly against the growing power of the state of Bohai (Balhae), which in 733 had actually launched a seaborne attack on Dengzhou in Shandong. Xuanzong also made progress on the empire's financial system by effectively registeri
1 CONC ng the population resulting in the higher tax revenues. This in turn increased the coffers of the government. He also repaired the Grand Canal system, which has fallen into disrepair during Wu Zetian's reign. Other areas such as C
1 CONC hinese arts and literature reached its zenith point during his reign with famous poets such as Li Bai, Du Fu and Meng Haoran who created some of the most elegant poems since the Han dynasty. He is famous, amongst other things, as 
1 CONC being a patron of the arts and for his notorious love affair with Yang Guifei. She was the young wife of his son Prince Shou (??), but he decreed their divorce and then entered her into a nunnery for a couple of years so that he c
1 CONC ould take her as his palace consort without shame. He rebuilt the ancient hot springs palace at the foot of Lishan Mountain for his consort and her sisters, naming it Huaqing Palace.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Later years
1 CONT As Xuanzong turned his attention to pleasure-seeking with Yang and her family, he paid less and less attention to the running of his empire, and much of his power fell into the hands of court officials like the corrupt but compete
1 CONC nt Li Linfu (who was succeeded by Yang's dissolute cousin Yang Guozhong), and the influential court eunuch Gao Lishi.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the meantime, the Jie Du Shi, (generals) of the outlying provinces (many of which had been recently reconquered) took more and more regional power into their own hands. One of these, a Turkish/Sogdian named An Lushan started th
1 CONC e An Lushan Rebellion in Fanyang in 755. The rebels captured the city of Luoyang and the capital Chang'an six months later.
1 CONT
1 CONT Xuanzong fled to Sichuan during the war, and Yang Guifei and Yang Guozhong, who attempted to flee with him, were killed by the Imperial army for their perceived part in the Emperor's weakness and loss of control.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Abdication and death
1 CONT Xuanzong abdicated his position to Suzong, the heir apparent in 756. He was put under house arrest in 760 in his own palace in support by his son and led by Li Fuguo. Still mourning for his lost Lady Yang, he died in 762 shortly b
1 CONC efore the rebellion was finally quashed. His rule would be the longest of the Tang dynasty, lasting nearly 44 years.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was deeply criticized by later historians even in Tang Dynasty for corruption and his appointment of Li Linfu to the chancellorship. On the other side, Mao Zedong said that Xuanzong was "half bright, half dark"(??????). The str
1 CONC ength that Xuanzong had allowed the warlords in the border provinces (Fanzhen) led to a period of increasing conflict and instability which set the stage for the end of the Tang Dynasty and the ensuing Five Dynasties and Ten Kingd
1 CONC oms Period.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also:
1 CONT
1 CONT minister Zhang Jiuling 
1 CONT artist Wu Tao-Tzu 
1 CONT royalty Princess Taiping 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Li Jiao Chancellor of China
1 CONT 710 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Song Jing 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Emperor Ruizong Emperor of Tang
1 CONT 712–756 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Emperor Suzong 
0 @N6000000001353874392@ NOTE Khosrow I, byname KHOSROW ANUSHIRVAN (Persian: "Khosrow of the Immortal Soul"), or KHOSROW THE JUST (d. AD 579), Persian king who ruled theSasanian empire from 531 to 579 and was remembered as a great reformer and patron of the ar
1 CONC ts and scholarship.
1 CONT Rise to power.
1 CONT Little is known of the early life of Khosrow beyond legends. One story says that when Khosrow's father, King Kavadh, took refuge with the Hephthalites, eastern neighbours of Iran, on the way (near the town ofNishapur) he married a
1 CONC  peasant's daughter, who gave birth to Khosrow.At his father's death Khosrow did not at first succeed him, but in a struggle for the throne he was successful and put to death his brothers. At the end of his father's reign, great s
1 CONC ocial disorders had occurred because of a religious revolution of a sect called the Mazdakites. Khosrow first restored order and then launched reforms to transform the declining Sasanian empire.
1 CONT Reforms.
1 CONT The reform of taxation was the most important of his actions, and it was probably copied from the Roman system inaugurated by the emperor Diocletian. Previously in the Sasanian empire taxes had been levied on the yield of land; Kh
1 CONC osrow established a fixed sum rather than a yearly variation. Other taxes were introduced that brought stability to the income of the state and were also fairer to those who paid the taxes. Khosrow's program of taxes lasted into I
1 CONC slamic times.
1 CONT Khosrow also reorganized the Sasanian bureaucracy, and the system of ministries, or divans, under a prime minister is said to have been initiated by him. He was fortunate during most of his reign in having a capable prime minister
1 CONC  called Bozorgmehr, who became famous in story and legend for his wisdom and abilities.
1 CONT Under Khosrow the process of decentralization of the power of the monarch was reversed, and the lower aristocracy, or knights, called dihqans, grew in importance at the expense of the great feudal lords, who had been more powerful
1 CONC  under Khosrow's predecessors. It is difficult toknow how many changes really can be attributed to Khosrow's reign andhow many are arbitrarily assigned to him because of his place in history. Whether the religiously sanctioned div
1 CONC ision of society into priests, warriors, bureaucracy, and common folk was codified under his reign, as claimed by some sources, is difficult to determine.
1 CONT Military campaigns.
1 CONT Khosrow also reorganized the army and appointed four chief commandersto guard the four frontiers of Iran. On the frontier against the Byzantines and their Arab allies in the Syrian Desert, against the peoplesof the steppes of sout
1 CONC hern Russia at the town of Derbent between the Caucasus Mountains and the Caspian Sea, and to the east of the sea in the present Turkmen steppes, Khosrow built defensive walls. The army, however, did not remain on the defensive, f
1 CONC or Khosrow's reign is notedfor his wars against the Byzantines. In 540 Antioch was conquered andheld for a short time by Khosrow. He brought many prisoners from Antioch and settled them in a new town near his capital of Ctesiphon,
1 CONC  modeled on old Antioch. In the east, Khosrow, in alliance with the Turks,a new power in Central Asia, crushed the Hephthalites, and he established a hegemony over many of their principalities. The Sasanian frontier in the east re
1 CONC ached the Amu Darya (Oxus River) during his reign.
1 CONT He also fought extensively in Armenia and Lazica in the Caucasus. Furthermore, under his reign a Sasanian army conquered Yemen. Khosrow relied on a professional army more than his predecessors, who had summoned feudal levies when 
1 CONC they set out to war.
1 CONT Patron of culture.
1 CONT Khosrow was also a great patron of culture, and in 529, when the ancient academy of Athens was closed, a number of Greek philosophers migrated to the Sasanian empire, where they were well received by the ruler. The later famous me
1 CONC dical school of Gondeshapur was probably startedin Khosrow's reign, and the famous physician Burzoe is supposed to have been sent to India by Khosrow to gather Sanskrit books of learning to be translated into the Middle Persian la
1 CONC nguage. The game of chess reportedly was also brought by him from India. Astronomy and astrology flourished at the court of Khosrow, and one star table (called the zij-i Shahriyar), which was the basis of many later Islamic tables
1 CONC , is said to have originated during the reign of Khosrow. Several works of Middle Persian, such as the Book of Deeds of Ardashir (Karnamak), are attributed to this period. Likewise, some scholars claim that the codification of the
1 CONC  Avesta, the sacred book of the Zoroastrian religion, as well as the creation of a special Avestan alphabet to record the text,took place at the order of Khosrow. Further, it is supposed that the stories and legends of ancient Ira
1 CONC n were gathered into a Khwatay-namak("Book of Kings") in the time of Khosrow and thus provided the sourcefor Ferdowsi's immortal epic much later. Some of the names found in Ferdowsi's Shah-nameh appear among the royal family of Kh
1 CONC osrow, which indicates at the least an interest on the part of the monarch in ancient legends.
1 CONT Perhaps more than fact, the stories told about Khosrow have made his name famous in history. Almost any pre-Islamic structure in Iran whoseorigin is unknown will be attributed to Khosrow by the simple folk. Undoubtedly he built ma
1 CONC ny bridges, roads, and palaces, but much more isassigned to him in legend. The famous palace with the huge arch, called Taq Kisra, in Ctesiphon, near modern Baghdad, is said to date from Khosrow I, but this is uncertain. Several c
1 CONC ollections of wise sayings of this monarch, as well as stories about him, have been preserved in Arabic or New Persian versions. In them his reputation for justice as well as wisdom is constantly cited. The splendour of the court 
1 CONC and theglory of his reign provided models for the later 'Abbasid court in Baghdad, and many of the institutions established by Khosrow were maintained in Islamic times, when Khosrow was hailed as the model pre-Islamic ruler to be 
1 CONC emulated by Muslim princes. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97, CHOSROES I]
1 CONT Chosroes I., "the blessed" (Anushirvan), A.D. 531-579, successor of Kavadh I., and the most famous of the Sassanid kings. At the beginning of his reign he concluded an "eternal" peace with the emperor Justinian, who wanted to be f
1 CONC ree for the conquest of Africa and Sicily. But successes against the Vandals and Goths caused Chosroes to renew the warin 540. He invaded Syria and took Antioch, and during the next years fought successfully in Lazica or Lazistan 
1 CONC (the ancient Colchis), on the Black sea, and in Mesopotamia. In 545 an armistice was concluded and in 562 a peace for 50 years in which the Persians left Lazistan to the Romans, and the Romans paid subsidies to Persia. Meanwhile i
1 CONC n the east the Hephthalites had been attacked by the Turks, who now appear for the first time in history. Chosroes united with them and conquered Bactria, while he left the country north of the Oxus to the Turks. He also assisted 
1 CONC the dynasts of Yemen to expel the Ethiopians and Yemen became dependent on Persia. In 571 a new war with Rome broke out about Armenia, in which Chosroes conquered the fortress Dara on the Euphrates, invaded Syria and Cappadocia, a
1 CONC nd returned with large booty. Duringthe negotiations with the emperor Tiberius, Chosroes died in 579, andwas succeeded by his son Hormizd IV.
1 CONT Although Chosroes had extirpated the heretical Persian sect of the Mazdakites he was a tolerant adherent of Zoroastrian orthodoxy. He introduced a system of taxation based upon a survey of landed possessions begun by his father. [
1 CONC Encyclopaedia Britannica, 1961ed., Vol. 5, p. 524B, KHOSROW I]
