0 @N6000000002187918554@ NOTE Pirooz
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Pirooz ("the winner") was son of Yazdgerd III the last Sassanid king of Persia. Most of what is known of Pirooz is written by his son, prince Narsieh.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Pirooz was very young at the time of the reign of his father Yazdgerd and never exercised the monarch power of the Sassanids. After the Arab conquest of Persia, Pirooz and much of the monarch family escaped through the Pami
1 CONC r mountains in what is now Tajikistan and arrived at Tang China, which was more supportive of the Sassanids.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the Old Book of Tang (???), Pirooz (???) asked for military help from Tang China against the Arabs in 661. The Tang court created the Governorate of Persia (?????) at what is now Zaranj, Afghanistan, with Pirooz as go
1 CONC vernor. Between 670 and 674, Pirooz arrived personally at the Tang court and was given the title of Youwuwei Jiangjun (?????, "Martial General and Guard of the Right [Flank]"). In 678, the Deputy Minister for Personnel (????) of t
1 CONC he Tang Court, Pei Xingjian (???) was ordered to escort Pirooz back to Persia. Pei Xingjian got as far as Suiye (??; near modern Tokmok, Kyrgyzstan) before returning, while Pirooz was forced to spend over 20 years in Tuhuoluo (???
1 CONC ; likely Bactra or Tokharistan) with several thousand followers. In 708 Pirooz arrived at the Tang court again and was given the title of Zuoweiwei Jiangjun (????? Awe-inspiring General and Guard of the Left [Flank]). He died soon
1 CONC  after, while some of his followers survived him.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the New Book of Tang (???), Pirooz died after receiving the title Youwuwei Jiangjun. It was then Pirooz's son, Narsieh (???), a hostage at the Tang court, who was escorted by Pei Xingjian westwards to Persia in 679 (n
1 CONC ot 678). Like the Old Book of Tang, Pei got as far as Suiye, and Narsieh (not Pirooz) then spent 20 years in Tuhuoluo. Finally, it was again Narsieh and not Pirooz that received the title of Zuoweiwei Jiangjun.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Old Book of Tang
1 CONT     * New Book of Tang
1 CONT     * China page
1 CONT     * Iranian.com
1 CONT     * Iranian.com
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Yazdgerd III Pretender Sassanid ruler
1 CONT - Succeeded by:
1 CONT -
0 @N6000000001353874421@ NOTE Ruled BET. 438 - 457 Sassanid King of Persia 1 2
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT Yazdegerd II (fl. 5th century), king of the Sasanian dynasty (reigned438-457), the son and successor of Bahram V.
1 CONT Although Yazdegerd was at first tolerant of the Christians, he remained a zealous Zoroastrian and later persecuted both Christians and Jews. He was engaged in a short war with Rome in 442 and also fought against the Kushans (Kusan
1 CONC as) and Kidarites in the east. Little else is known of Yazdegerd's reign; he was succeeded in turn by two of his sons,Hormizd III and Firuz. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97, YAZDEGERD II]
