0 @N6000000002187918722@ NOTE Bahram V
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Bahram V, King of Persia (421–438), also called "Bahram Gur", son of Yazdegerd I of Persia (399–421), after whose sudden death (or assassination) he gained the crown against the opposition of the grandees by the help of al-Mundhir
1 CONC , the Arabic dynast of al-Hirah. Bahram V's mother was Soshandukht, the daughter of the Jewish Exilarch.
1 CONT
1 CONT He promised to rule otherwise than his father, who had been very energetic and at the same time tolerant in religion. So Bahram V began a systematic persecution of the Christians, which led to a war with the Roman Empire. But he h
1 CONC ad little success, and soon concluded a treaty by which both empires promised toleration to the worshippers of the two rival religions, Christianity and Zoroastrianism.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 427 Bahram V crushed an invasion in the east by the nomadic Hephthalites, extending his influence into Central Asia, where his portrait survived for centuries on the coinage of Bukhara (in modern Uzbekistan).
1 CONT
1 CONT Bahram V deposed the vassal King of the Persian part of Armenia and made it a province. He is a great favourite in Persian tradition, which relates many stories of his valour and beauty, of his victories over the Romans, Turks, In
1 CONC dians and Africans, and of his adventures in hunting and in love; he is called Bahram Gur, "Onager," on account of his love for hunting, and in particular, hunting onagers.
1 CONT
1 CONT In reality Bahram V seems to have been rather a weak monarch, after the heart of the grandees and the priests. He is said to have built many great fire temples, with large gardens and villages (Tabari).
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Yazdegerd I Sassanid Ruler
1 CONT 421–438 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Yazdegerd II
0 @N6000000003051129316@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Bahram V", retrieved 15 Dec 07]
1 CONT Bahram V was the fourteenth Sassanid King of Persia (421-438). Also called Bahramgur, he was a son of Yazdegerd I (399-421), after whose sudden death (or assassination) he gained the crown against the opposition of the grandees by
1 CONC  the help of Mundhir, the Arabic dynast of al-Hirah. Bahram's mother was Soshandukht, the daughter of the Jewish Exilarch.
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign
1 CONT Bahram V began his reign with a systematic persecution of the Christians, (one of these Christians being Saint James Intercisus).
1 CONT
1 CONT War With Rome
1 CONT The persecution of James Intercisus led to a war with the Roman Empire in the year 420. The Romans sent their general Ardaburius with an extensive contingent into Armenia. Ardaburius defeated the Persian commander Narsehi and proc
1 CONC eeded to plunder the province of Arzanene and lay siege to Nisibis. Ardaburius abandoned the siege in the face of an advancing army under Bahram, who then besieged Theodosiopolis. After an abortive round of negotiations, the Persi
1 CONC ans were again defeated by Ardaburius and Procopius. Peace was then concluded between the Persians and Romans.
1 CONT
1 CONT Relations with Armenia
1 CONT The situation in Armenia occupied Bahram immediately after the conclusion of peace with Rome. Armenia had been without a king since Bahramgur's brother Shapur had vacated the country in 418. Bahramgur now desired that a descendant
1 CONC  of the royal line of kings, a scion of the Arsacids should be on the throne of Armenia. With this intention in mind, he selected an Arsacid named Artases, a son of Varahran-Shapur and made him King of Armenia with the royal title
1 CONC  Artaxerxes.
1 CONT
1 CONT But the newly appointed king did not have a favorable personal character. The frustrated nobles petitioned Bahramgur to remove Artases and admit Armenia into the Persian Empire so that the province would be under the direct contro
1 CONC l of the Sassanian Emperor. However, the annexation of Armenia by Persia was strongly opposed by the Armenian patriarch Isaac who felt the rule of a Christian better than that of a non-Christian regardless of his character or abil
1 CONC ity. Despite his strong protests, however, Armenia was still annexed by Bahramgur who placed it under the charge of a Persian Governor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Invasion of the Huns
1 CONT During the later part of Bahram V's reign, Persia was invaded from the north-east by Hephthalite hordes who ravaged northern Iran under the command of their Great Khan. They crossed the Elburz into Khorasan and proceeded as far as
1 CONC  the ancient town of Rei. Unprepared, Bahram initially made an offer or peace and submission which was well-received by the Khan of the Hephthalites. But crossing Tabaristan, Hyrcania and Nishapur by night, he took the Huns unawar
1 CONC es and massacred them along with their Khan and taking the Khan's wife hostage. The retreating Huns were pursued and slaughtered up to the Oxus. One of Bahram's generals followed the Huns deep into Hun territory and destroyed thei
1 CONC r power. His portrait which survived for centuries on the coinage of Bukhara (in contemporary Uzbekistan) is considered to be an evidence of his victory over the Huns.
1 CONT
1 CONT Legends associated with Bahramgur
1 CONT Numerous legends have been associated with Bahram. One account says that he aided an Indian king in his war against China and that, in return for his help, the Indian king made over the provinces of Makran and Sindh to Persia. The
1 CONC  Lurs of Persia, it is argued, are the descendants of musicians sent to Persia by the grateful Indian monarch. However there does not exist any historical proof in support of this story. Other accounts suggest that he married an I
1 CONC ndian princess. However, the conclusion of such a marriage alliance is regarded as highly dubious once again due to lack of evidence. His name is also associated with a legendary Indian prince of the Punjab.
1 CONT
1 CONT Legacy
1 CONT Bahram V has left behind a rich and colorful legacy which has survived to the present day. He is especially a favorite of the writers who have woven numerous legends and fantastical tales around him. His fame has survived the anni
1 CONC hilation of Zorostrianism and the Anti-Iranian measures of the Umayyads and the Mongols and many of the stories have been incorporated in contemporary Islamic lore.
1 CONT
1 CONT His legacy even survives outside Iran. He is believed to be the inspiration for the legend of Bahramgur prevalent in the Punjab.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is a great favourite in Persian tradition, which relates many stories of his valour and beauty, of his victories over the Romans, Turks, Indians and Africans, and of his adventures in hunting and in love; he is called Bahram Gu
1 CONC r, "Onager," on account of his love for hunting, and in particular, hunting onagers.
1 CONT
1 CONT Some have judged Bahram V to have been rather a weak monarch, after the heart of the grandees and the priests. He is said to have built many great fire-temples, with large gardens and villages (Tabari).
1 CONT
1 CONT Coins of Bahram V
1 CONT The coins of Bahram V are chiefly remarkable for their rude and coarse workmanship and for the number of the mints from which they were issued. The mint-marks include Ctesiphon, Ecbatana, Isaphan, Arbela, Ledan, Nehavend, Assyria,
1 CONC  Chuzistan, Media, and Kerman, or Carmania. The head-dress has the mural crown in front and behind, but interposes between these two detached fragments a crescent and a circle, emblems, no doubt, of the sun and moon gods. The reve
1 CONC rse shows the usual fire-altar, with guards, or attendants, watching it. The king's head appears in the flame upon the altar.
0 @N6000000002188002347@ NOTE Eudokia Palaiologina
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eudokia Palaiologina or Eudocia Palaeologina (Greek: ??d???a ?a?a???????a) (c. 1265 – 1302) was the third daughter of Byzantine Emperor Michael VIII Palaiologos and his wife, Theodora Doukaina Vatatzina, a grandniece of Emperor Jo
1 CONC hn III Doukas Vatatzes of Nicaea.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1282 Eudokia married in Constantinople John II Megaskomnenos, Emperor of Trebizond with whom she had two sons, Alexios and Michael. In 1298, after her husband's death and ascension of her son Alexios II, she took the younger so
1 CONC n with her and returned to her brother's court at Constantinople.
1 CONT
1 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos received his sister well, promising to send her back whenever she wished to her elder son, whose position — such was the official Byzantine version — he did all in his power to consolidate.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eudokia had become entirely devoted to Trapezuntine interests. She refused a second marriage with King Stefan Uroš II Milutin of Serbia, as she wished to keep her troth to her first consort, and secretly opposed her brother’s plan
1 CONC  of marrying Alexios II to a daughter of the high court official Nikephoros Choumnos. She pretended, however, to approve this match, so that, under pretence of making arrangements for her son’s wedding, she might return to Trebizo
1 CONC nd.
1 CONT
1 CONT Meanwhile, Alexios II decided for himself to marry an Iberian princess. His uncle Andronikos II wanted this marriage annulled, so Eudokia, on the pretext of inducing her son to dissolve the marriage, succeeded in returning to Treb
1 CONC izond in 1301, where she advised her son to keep his Iberian wife. Eudokia died in the following year, and was perhaps buried in the church of Saint Gregory.
0 @N6000000001353874442@ NOTE 421 in [murdered] Khorasan 1
1 CONT Event: Ruled BET. 399 - 421 Sassanid King of Persia 2 3
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT Yasdegerd. Izdegerdes, "made by God."
1 CONT ----------
1 CONT Yazdegerd I, son of Shapur III, 399-421, called "the sinner" by the Persians, was a highly intelligent ruler, who tried to emancipate himself from the dominion of the magnates and the Magian priests. He punished the nobles severel
1 CONC y when they attempted oppression; he stopped the persecution of the Christians and granted them their own organization.With the Roman empire he lived in peace and friendship, and is therefore as much praised by the Byzantine autho
1 CONC rs as he is blamed by the Persians. After a reign of 20 years he appears to have been murdered in Khurasan. [Encyclopaedia Britannica, 1961 ed., Vol. 23, p. 880, YAZDEGERD
1 CONT ----------
1 CONT Yazdegerd I (fl. 5th century), king of the Sasanian Empire (reigned 399-420).
1 CONT Yazdegerd was a highly intelligent ruler who tried to emancipate himself from the dominion of the magnates and of the Magi (a priestly caste serving a number of religions); thus, his reign is viewed differently by Christian and Ma
1 CONC gian sources. Because he stopped the persecutionof the Christians, the Christian writers praise his clemency, but thesources dependent on Magian tradition refer to him as "Yazdegerd the Sinner." He also tried to limit the power of
1 CONC  the nobles, but their resistance finally was answered with severity. He lived in peace and friendship with the Roman Empire and was therefore praised by Byzantine authors. He appears to have been murdered in Khorasan and was succ
1 CONC eeded by one of his sons, Bahram V. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97, YAZDEGERD]
