0 @N6000000002187918744@ NOTE Yazdegerd I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Yazdegerd I ("made by God" Izdigerdes), King of Persia (399–421), son of Shapur III of Persia (383–388)
1 CONT
1 CONT Yazdegerd I was a highly intelligent ruler, who tried to emancipate himself from the dominion of the magnates and the Magian priests. He punished the nobles severely when they attempted oppression; he granted Christians their own 
1 CONC organization.
1 CONT
1 CONT With the Roman Empire Yazdegerd I lived in peace and friendship, and is therefore as much praised by the Byzantine authors
1 CONT
1 CONT After a reign of twenty-two years he appears to have been murdered in Khorasan.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Bahram IV Sassanid Ruler
1 CONT 399–421 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Bahram V
0 @N6000000003051336683@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Yazdegerd I", retrieved 15 Dec 07]
1 CONT Yazdegerd I ("made by God" Izdigerdes) was thirteenth Sassanid King of Persia (399?421), son of Shapur III of Persia (383-388) succeeded Bahram IV as the King of Persia and ruled from 399 to 420.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the early part of his reign, he was entrusted by Arcadius, the Roman Emperor of the East with the care of his son Theodosius. Flattered, he performed his newfound role with utmost sincerity providing him the best possible educa
1 CONC tion and assistance.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yazdegerd I was a wise, benevolant, and astute ruler. He was also known for his religious tolerance, towards both Christians and Jews. For example, the Talmud (Ksubos, 61a) relates that Ameimar, Rav Ashi, and Mar Zutra would sit i
1 CONC n his court. However, excessive zeal of the Christian bishop of Ctesiphon, Abdaas, provoked a reaction, and when he tried to burn the Great Fire temple of Ctesiphon,Yazdegerd I turned against the Christians. Christian churches all
1 CONC  over the nation were destroyed and Christians driven off from the kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the year 413, Varahran-Shapur, the King of Armenia died and the crown passed on to a boy of ten. Yazdegerd I released Khusro whom Bahram IV had imprisoned in the Castle of Oblivion and reinstated him upon the throne of Armenia 
1 CONC in order to stabilize the condition. But Khusro died soon afterwards and Armenia fell into chaos once again. Yazdegerd I responded by placing his own son Shapur on the throne of Armenia. When Yazdegerd I was overcome by mortal ill
1 CONC ness in the year 419, Shapur immediately rushed to Ctesiphon to claim the Sassanian throne. But in the battle of succession that followed, Shapur was squarely defeated by Yazdegerd's other son, Bahram, who ascended the throne as B
1 CONC ahram V. Yazdegerd I meanwhile died soon afterwards.
